good evening the near collapse of  equitable life brought home to millions  of policyholders what happens when an  insurer can't make good on its promises  and whilst it doesn't attract as much  attention as Banking Regulation  Insurance Regulation is vital to the  economists smooth running well the man  in charge of that is Sam Woods deputy  governor of the Bank of England and  chief executive of the Prudential  regulatory authority he joins me now for  his first broadcast interview Sam great  to see thank you for joining me now  while everyone knows that the bank  oversees and supervises the banking set  a few people may necessarily know that  you oversee insurance why is it so  important this is a huge sector here in  the UK two point four trillion in assets  which to give you a view as a sense is  bigger than GDP and today we're  announcing a series of reforms to the  way that we regulate that sector which  basically are about cutting bureaucracy  making life will be easier for the  smaller firms but this is no bonfire the  regulation and the reason for that is  directly to your question the huge  numbers of people depend on this  industry in one example is 27 million  people in this country have a motor  insurance policy  another is six million people are  relying on these companies to pay their  pensions in a moment so while we are  going to make the regulation more  efficient we're not gonna go soft in  these people at risk now insurance is  one of those sectors in which Britain  punches above its weight globally how do  you balance the need to retain that  whilst being fairly tough with the  insurance companies well the first thing  to say is industry in this country is  alive and well and the best evidence for  that is that returns are healthier at 9%  return on equity for the major insurers  the insurance sector is grown by 10%  over the last couple of years so I think  there's absolutely no evidence to  suggest that the weight of our  regulation is somehow impeding the  competitiveness of the UK insurance  sector but one thing we do have to have  an ID to of course is smaller firms and  that we're being proportionate in terms  of how we apply our regulation to them  and one of the things we've said today  is that we will cut the reporting burden  on those firms by up to half that may  mean though that insurance some some  lines may be less aggressively regulated  than they were previously I I wouldn't  say any more regulation is aggressive  but it is all meant  be robust and the reason for that is  that people depend on these services in  a very vital way I spent quite a lot of  time with policyholders over the last  few months to remind myself of that and  when you talk to people about what they  rely on insurance companies to do you  realize it's very important they're  there to make good when things go wrong  and I mean you've touched on this in the  past in some of your speeches you've  seen some really quite emotional cases  at times of where insurance companies  have done the right thing but they could  only do that because they were being  properly supervised yes well you know  when you hear these cases here  particularly when people have a critical  illness or in a some kind of terrible  accident and you realize you know the  the kind of emotional value of what  insurance companies are doing in those  situations you guys is very important  that we pay attention to our job which  simply put is to make sure the insurance  companies have got enough money to pay  out when things go wrong and of course  all being the case that is hopefully  that'll be the way it continues now what  about competition in the sex this is  something that the Treasury select  committee has been looking at they would  like to see you encouraging competition  more what's it what's your response to  that I mean it's competition losing out  right now  well so I don't think so and you know  there are many points of agreement  between us in the Treasury select  committee I think it's a great thing  that Nicky Morgan and that committee  have decided to look at this point of  regulation which is you say often goes  much less noticed than banking but there  is a slight difference of view about  whether we should make the competition a  primary objective of the Prudential  Regulation Authority and I'm not very  enthusiastic about that for two reasons  one is to be honest the most important  contribution we can make to competition  is as a referee so setting the rules for  everyone so that unscrupulous firms  can't come in and undercut the  well-managed firms that's our core job  and secondly the benefit of focus I  think the lesson of the financial crisis  in this country is if you've got a  regulator with ten objectives they  probably won't do anything very well  worth pointing out though I guess that's  if you look at things like motor  insurance premiums have been coming down  very sharply in recent years suggest  that there isn't an awful lot wrong with  competition well I think there's been a  pretty ferocious competition in that  market in particular and that's been  including through air players coming in  from outside the you  and it's true that we generally don't  think there's a major competition  problem in insurance markets another way  to look at that is we've authorized 26  new insurers over the last four years  seems to me that's a reasonably healthy  rate what about insolvency - now this is  the pan-european here wide Insurance  Regulation directive what happens post  brexit will that stay in place for  British insurance well in that is a very  good question - which none of us  currently know the answer and it will  depend of course on what kind of a deal  the government wants to strike and you  know we're looking forward to what the  prime minister says on Friday so the one  point I would make though is that it's  been an enormous effort to bring in this  new regulation we're now in the process  of tidying it up but I think if you ask  the insurance companies do they want to  embark on another completely new  regulatory journey at this point most of  them would say no right all right well  amen to that I'm sure if any the  insured's are watching some words great  to say thank thank you very much
 

